# {{.Name}} ({{.Type}})

Store external storage credentials in MotherDuck under the connected SQL
account. A different service account needs its own secret when it accesses
that storage.

## Usage and lifecycle

Prefer structured `params` for ordinary secrets. Use `secret_sql` only for
advanced clauses supported by the service. It is a secret body fragment, not
a complete `CREATE SECRET` statement. Do not include semicolons or repeat
`TYPE` and `PROVIDER` clauses already modeled by the resource.

Updating the body uses `CREATE OR REPLACE SECRET`. Renaming requires resource
replacement. MotherDuck stores secret names in lowercase, so use lowercase
names to keep plans and imports readable. Destroy drops the named secret and
succeeds when it is already gone.

The computed `scope` attribute is the scope list as DuckDB reports it, for
example `[mybucket]`. When `params` sets `scope`, planning renders the
configured value the same way and plans an update only when the live scope
differs.

MotherDuck redacts secret values on reads. Values set in `params` and
`flight_params` are still stored in Terraform state and saved plans, so protect
both.

## Keep values out of state

With Terraform 1.11 or later, set the values in `params_wo` or
`flight_params_wo` instead. Terraform sends these write-only arguments to
MotherDuck and stores them in neither the plan nor the state. They take the
same keys as `params` and `flight_params`, and each cannot be combined with its
stored form.

Terraform cannot detect a change in a value it does not store. Set
`params_wo_version` to a number and change it whenever the values change. That
change plans an in-place update, which writes the current values. To move an
existing secret, replace `params` with `params_wo`, add `params_wo_version`,
and apply. The update rewrites the secret and removes the values from state.
Earlier state versions kept by the backend still contain them.

{{tffile "examples/resources/motherduck_secret/write_only/main.tf"}}

Import recovers public metadata, not credential values. Supply the intended
secret body deliberately before a rotation or update. See
[state and lifecycle](../guides/state-and-lifecycle.md).

## Flights secrets

A `flights` secret holds key and value pairs that a Flight reads as environment
variables. Set its values in `flight_params`, which the provider sends as
`PARAMS MAP {...}`. `params` is not valid for this type. Attach the secret to a
Flight with `flight_secret_names`. The run receives each pair twice, as `<key>`
and as `<secret name>_<key>`. MotherDuck sets `MOTHERDUCK_TOKEN`,
`MOTHERDUCK_FLIGHTS_RUN`, `MOTHERDUCK_FLIGHT_ID`, `MOTHERDUCK_FLIGHT_RUN_ID`, and
`MOTHERDUCK_HOST` itself, so a key with one of those names is available only
under its prefixed name. MotherDuck lists the keys of a `flights` secret but
always redacts the values.

